[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says the reports about wrong dates are one bug with one fix queued.
They are three. Each has its own ticket and its own fix, and fixing one leaves the other two in place.

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {issues} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. <code>parser</code> is fixed (<code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
locales</code>). <code>hint</code> is won't fix: the <code>booking-v3</code> form is retired.
<code>one-day-move</code> is still open: neither change touches it.
</Callout>

| problem | status | ticket |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | fixed, 5 Oct | closed as completed |
| `hint` | won't fix: `booking-v3` retired | closed as not planned |
| `one-day-move` | open, no fix yet | open |

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day
first. A booking for 7 April comes out as 4 July. This holds for the app, email and the
`booking-v2` form.

**`hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells people to type month first.** Its date field prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under it, then reads what people type the tenant's way, day first. The people follow the
hint and get the same flip as `parser`, through a different bug: the parser fix does not touch it.
Only form reports from `booking-v3` tenants land here.

**`one-day-move`: dates move by one day, not by a day/month flip.** Tuesday shows as Monday; an
invite arrives before the booking; one meeting lands on two days for clients in Lisbon and Toronto;
only June is off. No locale or form explains this, so it is not a parser problem. It looks like a time
zone or daylight-saving shift. The desk had already marked these reports in `desk-notes.json` and
footnoted them under `parser`; here they are a problem of their own.

## How the split was made

- Each report is counted once by its id. The export lists some twice.
- The problem comes from the tenant, not the report: its locale (asked of the platform) and its
  booking form, as in `causes.ts`. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, and the table
  shows how often it was wrong.
- Every report with a date pair in its body was checked: all but the one-day moves are a day/month
  swap, and every weekday named matches its date.

## Why one ticket hid three

Reports were merged by subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). Subjects
like "Date is wrong" fit all three problems, so the merge could not tell them apart. The subject
says how it felt; the tenant and the channel say what broke.

